1. src/filelock/_soft.py:27 - File created with default mode 0o644 (world-readable) without explicit secure permissions
2. src/filelock/_unix.py:44 - File created with default mode 0o644, with attempted fchmod fix that can silently fail due to PermissionError suppression
3. src/filelock/_windows.py:28 - File created with default mode 0o644 (world-readable) without explicit secure permissions

All three instances use the default mode of 0o644 which is overly permissive for lock files. Lock files should use mode 0o600 (owner read+write only) to prevent unauthorized access. The vulnerability allows other users on the system to read the lock file contents, potentially leaking sensitive information.
